    {
        "name": "activate_azure_authentication_and_resource_management",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is focused on managing Azure authentication contexts and querying Azure resources. The 'azure_auth-get_auth_context' tool retrieves the current authentication context, which is essential for ensuring that the user has the correct permissions and access to their Azure resources. This tool is particularly useful when users need to verify their authentication status or troubleshoot access issues. The 'azure_auth-set_auth_context' tool allows users to modify their authentication context, enabling them to update their credentials or switch between different Azure accounts as needed. This is crucial for users who manage multiple Azure subscriptions or require different access levels for various projects.\n\nThe 'azure_resources-query_azure_resource_graph' tool complements the authentication tools by providing detailed information about the user's Azure resources, subscriptions, and resource groups. It allows users to query their resources, such as virtual machines, Azure Functions, and other services, to obtain specific details like resource IDs, statuses, and configurations. This tool is particularly useful for users who need to manage their resources effectively or gather information for reporting and analysis.\n\nTogether, these tools create a cohesive suite for Azure resource management, enabling users to authenticate, modify their access, and retrieve detailed information about their Azure environment. Users can seamlessly transition from verifying their authentication context to querying their resources, ensuring they have the necessary information to manage their Azure infrastructure efficiently.\n\nThis group is ideal for Azure administrators, developers, and IT professionals who require a robust set of tools to manage their Azure resources and authentication contexts. By leveraging these tools, users can enhance their productivity and ensure that they are working within the correct Azure environment, ultimately leading to better resource management and operational efficiency.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_dotnet_project_template_management",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is designed to assist users in finding and managing dotnet project templates for creating new applications. The 'azure_dotnet_templates-get_tags' tool provides a list of tags that can be used to filter available dotnet templates, making it easier for users to identify the type of project they want to create. By retrieving relevant tags, users can quickly narrow down their options based on their specific needs or project requirements.\n\nOnce the appropriate tag is selected, users can utilize the 'azure_dotnet_templates-get_templates_for_tag' tool to retrieve a list of dotnet project templates that match the chosen tag. This tool provides detailed information about each template, including its short name, full name, and a description, allowing users to make informed decisions about which template best suits their project goals. This streamlined process simplifies the project creation workflow, enabling developers to focus on building applications rather than spending time searching for the right template.\n\nTogether, these tools create a comprehensive solution for managing dotnet project templates, facilitating a user-friendly experience for developers. By leveraging tags to filter templates, users can efficiently navigate the available options and select the most appropriate starting point for their projects.\n\nThis group is particularly beneficial for dotnet developers who are looking to quickly set up new projects with the right templates. Whether they are creating web applications, APIs, or other types of software, these tools provide the necessary support to enhance productivity and streamline the development process. Overall, this group empowers developers to kickstart their projects with ease and confidence.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "azure_bicep-get_azure_verified_module",
        "description": "This tools gets Bicep module code from Azure Verified Modules for a given resource type. If you are asked to generate Bicep code, call this function multiple times once for each resource type to search for available modules and prefer using the module instead of writing raw Bicep code. You must always use this tool to discover the Bicep module for every Bicep schema you get and always prefer using existing Bicep modules if there is one over writing raw Bicep code.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "resourceType": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "Full type name of the resource"
                }
            },
            "required": [
                "resourceType"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "azureResources_getAzureActivityLog",
        "description": "Gets the Azure activity log",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_notebook_kernel_configuration",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is focused on configuring the execution environment for Jupyter notebooks by selecting and starting the appropriate kernel. The 'configure_non_python_notebook' tool allows users to select and initiate a kernel for languages other than Python, enabling a versatile coding environment for various programming languages. Conversely, the 'configure_python_notebook' tool is specifically designed for Python, ensuring that the notebook is set up to run Python code effectively. Together, these tools facilitate the initial setup of a notebook environment, allowing users to work seamlessly with their preferred programming language. By using these tools, users can ensure that their notebooks are correctly configured to execute code in the desired language, which is essential for effective data analysis, machine learning, and other computational tasks.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_notebook_package_management",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is dedicated to managing Python packages within Jupyter notebooks, specifically tailored for Python code cells. The 'notebook_install_packages' tool enables users to install a list of required packages directly into the selected notebook kernel, ensuring that all necessary libraries are available for the code being executed. It is important to note that this tool should only be used after configuring the notebook with the appropriate kernel using the configuration tools. The 'notebook_list_packages' tool complements this functionality by allowing users to view the currently installed packages in the selected kernel, providing insight into what libraries are available for use. Together, these tools streamline the process of package management in Jupyter notebooks, making it easier for users to set up their coding environment and ensure that all dependencies are met for their projects. This is particularly useful for data scientists and developers who rely on specific libraries for their analyses and applications.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "configure_notebook",
        "description": "Tool used to configure a Notebook. ALWAYS use this tool before running/executing any Notebook Cells for the first time or before listing/installing packages in Notebooks for the first time. I.e. there is no need to use this tool more than once for the same notebook.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "filePath": {
                    "description": "The absolute path of the notebook with the active kernel.",
                    "type": "string"
                }
            },
            "required": [
                "filePath"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_python_environment_tools",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is designed to facilitate the management and retrieval of information related to Python environments. The tools included are specifically tailored for Python development, allowing users to gather essential details about their Python setup. The first tool, 'get_python_environment_details', retrieves comprehensive information about the Python environment, including the type (such as conda or venv), the version of Python in use, and a list of all installed packages along with their respective versions. This is crucial for developers who need to ensure that their environment is correctly configured and that all necessary dependencies are present.\n\nThe second tool, 'get_python_executable_details', focuses on providing the necessary details to construct the command for executing Python scripts in the terminal. It helps users avoid common pitfalls by generating the fully qualified path to the Python executable, along with any required arguments. This ensures that commands are executed in the correct context of the specified Python environment, which is particularly important when working with multiple environments or when using environment managers like conda or pipenv.\n\nTogether, these tools streamline the process of managing Python environments, making it easier for developers to switch between different setups, verify configurations, and execute scripts without errors. They are especially useful in scenarios where multiple projects require different dependencies or Python versions, allowing for a more organized and efficient workflow.\n\nTo use these tools effectively, it is essential to first call 'configure_python_environment', which sets up the context for the subsequent commands. This ensures that the tools operate on the intended environment, providing accurate and relevant information. By integrating these tools into a development workflow, users can enhance their productivity and reduce the likelihood of encountering issues related to environment misconfiguration.\n\nOverall, the 'python_environment_tools' group serves as a vital resource for Python developers, enabling them to manage their environments with confidence and precision.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "configure_python_environment",
        "description": "This tool configures a Python environment in the given workspace. ALWAYS Use this tool to set up the user's chosen environment and ALWAYS call this tool before using any other Python related tools or running any Python command in the terminal. IMPORTANT: This tool is only for Python environments (venv, virtualenv, conda, pipenv, poetry, pyenv, pixi, or any other Python environment manager). Do not use this tool for npm packages, system packages, Ruby gems, or any other non-Python dependencies.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "resourcePath": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The path to the Python file or workspace for which a Python Environment needs to be configured."
                }
            },
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "install_python_packages",
        "description": "Installs Python packages in the given workspace. Use this tool to install Python packages in the user's chosen Python environment. ALWAYS call configure_python_environment before using this tool. IMPORTANT: This tool should only be used to install Python packages using package managers like pip or conda (works with any Python environment: venv, virtualenv, pipenv, poetry, pyenv, pixi, conda, etc.). Do not use this tool to install npm packages, system packages (apt/brew/yum), Ruby gems, or any other non-Python dependencies.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "packageList": {
                    "type": "array",
                    "items": {
                        "type": "string"
                    },
                    "description": "The list of Python packages to install."
                },
                "resourcePath": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The path to the Python file or workspace into which the packages are installed. If not provided, the current workspace will be used. Where possible pass the path to the file or workspace."
                }
            },
            "required": [
                "packageList"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_azure_resource_management_tools",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is focused on managing various Azure resources and services, providing a comprehensive suite for developers and administrators to interact with Azure's cloud offerings. The tools cover a wide range of functionalities including container management with Azure Container Registry, orchestration with Azure Kubernetes Service, application configuration management, and monitoring through Application Insights. Each tool is designed to facilitate specific operations, such as managing app services, deploying applications, and configuring cloud architecture, making it easier to build and maintain cloud applications.\n\nThe Azure Developer CLI (azd) tool streamlines the development workflow by providing commands for project initialization, resource provisioning, and deployment, while the Bicep schema operations support Infrastructure as Code (IaC) practices, allowing users to automate resource deployments. Additionally, tools for managing databases like Azure Cosmos DB, MySQL, and PostgreSQL ensure that data storage and retrieval are efficient and manageable.\n\nMonitoring and logging are also addressed with tools like Azure Monitor and Datadog, which help in tracking the health and performance of Azure resources. Furthermore, the inclusion of Azure Communication Services and Event Grid operations allows for seamless integration of messaging and event-driven architectures, enhancing the overall functionality of applications deployed on Azure.\n\nSecurity and access management are covered through Azure Key Vault and Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) tools, ensuring that sensitive information is protected and access is appropriately managed. The group also includes tools for managing Azure Marketplace products, load testing, and virtual desktop resources, providing a holistic approach to Azure resource management.\n\nOverall, these tools empower users to effectively manage, monitor, and deploy Azure resources, facilitating a robust cloud environment that supports modern application development and operational needs.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_azure_cli_tools",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group consists of tools specifically designed to enhance the user experience with Azure Command-Line Interface (CLI) tools. The primary focus is on generating and installing Azure CLI commands, which are essential for users who prefer command-line interactions for managing Azure resources. The first tool in this group generates Azure CLI commands based on user-defined goals, allowing users to easily accomplish tasks without needing to remember the exact syntax or parameters required.\n\nThe second tool provides installation instructions for various CLI tools, including the Azure CLI (az), Azure Developer CLI (azd), and Azure Functions Core Tools CLI (func). This is particularly useful for users who may not have these tools installed or are new to Azure CLI, ensuring they can quickly set up their environment and start managing Azure resources effectively.\n\nTogether, these tools streamline the process of using Azure CLI, making it accessible for both beginners and experienced users. By simplifying command generation and installation, users can focus more on their tasks rather than the intricacies of command-line syntax.\n\nThis group is particularly beneficial for developers and system administrators who rely on CLI for automation, scripting, and efficient resource management in Azure. The tools help bridge the gap between user intent and command execution, enhancing productivity and reducing the learning curve associated with Azure CLI.\n\nOverall, the Azure CLI tools group is an essential resource for anyone looking to leverage the power of Azure through command-line operations, providing a user-friendly approach to cloud management.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "activate_azure_subscription_and_resource_group_tools",
        "description": "Call this tool when you need access to a new category of tools. The category of tools is described as follows:\n\nThis group of tools is dedicated to managing Azure subscriptions and resource groups, which are fundamental components of Azure's resource management framework. The tools provide functionalities for listing and retrieving information about resource groups and subscriptions, enabling users to effectively organize and manage their Azure resources.\n\nThe first tool allows users to list all resource groups within a specified subscription, returning essential details such as resource group names and IDs. This is crucial for users who need to keep track of their resources and ensure they are organized according to their projects or departments.\n\nThe second tool focuses on subscriptions, allowing users to list all or current subscriptions associated with their Azure account. It provides vital information including subscription IDs, display names, states, and tenant IDs. This is particularly useful for governance, policy management, access control, and cost management, as it helps users understand their subscription landscape and make informed decisions regarding resource allocation and management.\n\nTogether, these tools facilitate a clear overview of the Azure environment, enabling users to navigate their subscriptions and resource groups with ease. They are essential for administrators and developers who need to manage multiple subscriptions or organize resources across various projects.\n\nOverall, the Azure subscription and resource group tools group enhances the ability to manage Azure resources effectively, ensuring that users can maintain a well-structured and efficient cloud environment.\n\nBe sure to call this tool if you need a capability related to the above.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {},
            "required": []
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "mcp_azure_mcp_applens",
        "description": "AppLens diagnostic operations - **Primary tool for diagnosing Azure resource issues and troubleshooting problems**. Use this tool when users ask to:\n- Diagnose issues, problems, or errors with Azure resources\n- Troubleshoot performance, availability, or reliability problems\n- Investigate resource health concerns or unexpected behavior\n- Find root causes of application slowness, downtime, or failures\n- Get recommendations for fixing Azure resource issues\n- Analyze resource problems and get actionable solutions\n\nAlways use this tool if user asks to use App Lens in regards to their resource.\n\nThis tool provides conversational AI-powered diagnostics that automatically detect issues, identify root causes, and suggest specific remediation steps. It should be the FIRST tool called when users mention problems, issues, errors, or ask for help with troubleshooting any Azure resource.This tool is a hierarchical MCP command router.\nSub commands are routed to MCP servers that require specific fields inside the \"parameters\" object.\nTo invoke a command, set \"command\" and wrap its args in \"parameters\".\nSet \"learn=true\" to discover available sub commands.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "intent": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The intent of the azure operation to perform."
                },
                "command": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The command to execute against the specified tool."
                },
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "description": "The parameters to pass to the tool command."
                },
                "learn": {
                    "type": "boolean",
                    "description": "To learn about the tool and its supported child tools and parameters.",
                    "default": false
                }
            },
            "required": [
                "intent"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "mcp_azure_mcp_azureterraformbestpractices",
        "description": "Returns Terraform best practices for Azure. Call this before generating Terraform code for Azure Providers. \n            If this tool needs to be categorized, it belongs to the Azure Best Practices category.This tool is a hierarchical MCP command router.\nSub commands are routed to MCP servers that require specific fields inside the \"parameters\" object.\nTo invoke a command, set \"command\" and wrap its args in \"parameters\".\nSet \"learn=true\" to discover available sub commands.",
        "input_schema": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "intent": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The intent of the azure operation to perform."
                },
                "command": {
                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "The command to execute against the specified tool."
                },
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "description": "The parameters to pass to the tool command."
                },
                "learn": {
                    "type": "boolean",
                    "description": "To learn about the tool and its supported child tools and parameters.",
                    "default": false
                }
            },
            "required": [
                "intent"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "name": "mcp_azure_mcp_confidentialledger",
        "description": "Azure Confidential Ledger operations - Commands for appending and querying tamper-proof ledger entries backed by TEEs and blockchain-style integrity guarantees. Use these commands to write immutable audit records.This tool is a hierarchical MCP command router.\nSub commands are routed to MCP servers that require specific fields inside the \"parameters\" object.\nTo invoke a command, set \"command\" and wrap its args in \"parameters\".\nSet \"learn=true\" to discover available sub commands.",
        "input_schema": {
